## Database

### 1. Overview

The project uses **PostgreSQL** as its Relational Database Management System (RDBMS).

The database is responsible for storing and managing all academic, administrative, and user-related information. It is designed to ensure data integrity, consistency, security, and efficient data retrieval.

**Database Name:** `university_management`

**Database Management System:** PostgreSQL

**ORM:** Spring Data JPA / Hibernate

**Database Type:** Relational Database

### 2. Database Tables

The database consists of the following main tables:

#### 2.1. Roles

Stores the different roles available within the system.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique role identifier |
| name | VARCHAR(50) | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | Role name (STUDENT, TEACHER, ADMIN) |
| description | TEXT | NULL | Role description |

#### 2.2. Users

Stores information about all system users, including students, teachers, and administrators.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique user identifier |
| first_name | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL | User's first name |
| last_name | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL | User's last name |
| email | VARCHAR(150) | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | User's email address |
| password | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL | Hashed user password |
| role_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Roles(id) |
| phone | VARCHAR(20) | NULL | User's phone number |
| created_at | TIMESTAMP | NOT NULL | Account creation date |

#### 2.3. Courses

Stores information about the courses offered by the university.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique course identifier |
| name | VARCHAR(150) | NOT NULL | Course name |
| description | TEXT | NULL | Course description |
| teacher_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| semester | VARCHAR(50) | NULL | Academic semester |
| academic_year | VARCHAR(20) | NULL | Academic year |

#### 2.4. Enrollments

Manages student registrations in university courses.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique enrollment identifier |
| student_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| course_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Courses(id) |
| enrollment_date | DATE | NOT NULL | Registration date |
| status | VARCHAR(30) | NOT NULL | Enrollment status |

A unique constraint should be applied to the combination of `student_id` and `course_id` to prevent duplicate registrations.

#### 2.5. Grades

Stores the grades obtained by students in different courses.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique grade identifier |
| student_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| teacher_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| course_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Courses(id) |
| value | DECIMAL(5,2) | NOT NULL | Grade obtained |
| assessment_type | VARCHAR(50) | NULL | Exam, assignment, practical work, etc. |
| publication_date | TIMESTAMP | NULL | Date the grade is published |

#### 2.6. Absences

Records and manages student absences.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique absence identifier |
| student_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| course_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Courses(id) |
| teacher_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| absence_date | DATE | NOT NULL | Date of absence |
| justification | TEXT | NULL | Absence justification |
| status | VARCHAR(30) | NOT NULL | JUSTIFIED or UNJUSTIFIED |

#### 2.7. Timetable

Stores the scheduled university sessions and their corresponding information.

| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |

| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY | Unique session identifier |
| course_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Courses(id) |
| teacher_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY | References Users(id) |
| session_date | DATE | NOT NULL | Session date |
| start_time | TIME | NOT NULL | Session starting time |
| end_time | TIME | NOT NULL | Session ending time |
| room | VARCHAR(50) | NOT NULL | Classroom or room |
| session_type | VARCHAR(30) | NULL | Lecture, practical work, tutorial |

### 3. Database Relationships

The database follows a relational structure with the following relationships:

| Relationship | Cardinality | Description |

| Roles - Users | One-to-Many | A role can be assigned to multiple users. |
| Users - Courses | One-to-Many | A teacher can be responsible for multiple courses. |
| Users - Enrollments | One-to-Many | A student can enroll in multiple courses. |
| Courses - Enrollments | One-to-Many | A course can have multiple enrolled students. |
| Users - Grades | One-to-Many | A student can receive multiple grades. |
| Courses - Grades | One-to-Many | A course can contain multiple grades. |
| Users - Absences | One-to-Many | A student can have multiple recorded absences. |
| Courses - Absences | One-to-Many | A course can have multiple recorded absences. |
| Courses - Timetable | One-to-Many | A course can have multiple scheduled sessions. |
| Users - Timetable | One-to-Many | A teacher can have multiple scheduled sessions. |

### 4. Database Constraints and Integrity

The database uses several constraints to maintain data consistency:

- **Primary Keys (PK):** Uniquely identify each record.
- **Foreign Keys (FK):** Maintain relationships between tables.
- **NOT NULL:** Ensure that required fields contain values.
- **UNIQUE:** Prevent duplicate values, particularly for user email addresses and student-course enrollments.
- **CHECK:** Validate values such as grades and session times.
- **Referential Integrity:** Prevent invalid references between related records.

